{
  "id": "bacterial-persister-challenge-second-biology",
  "name": "Reversible bacterial persister switching under antibiotic challenge",
  "description": "A reversible phenotype mixture produces a fast killing phase and a slower surviving tail. This is transient persistence, not genetic antibiotic resistance, and does not infer individual-cell survival probabilities. Wild-type antibiotic Table1 rates perhour: normal-4,persister-0.4,forward1.2e-6,reverse0.1. Initial normal0.9999994,persister6e-7 and horizon8 are illustrative normalized population choices. RK4step0.002 resolves both decay modes. https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1449587/ Signed net-rate flows encode the stated differential equations. No clamps or resets. These educational equations are not fitted biological forecasts.",
  "source": "https://doi.org/10.1534/genetics.104.035352",
  "reference": "Kussell, Kishony, Balaban and Leibler (2005), Bacterial persistence: a model of survival in changing environments, Genetics169:1807-1814, Eq1-3 and Table1; Balaban et al (2004), doi:10.1126/science.1099390.",
  "category": "biology",
